<title>MEXICO: Mexico hog farmers scold gov't over U.S. pressure.</title>
<headline>Mexico hog farmers scold gov't over U.S. pressure.</headline>
<dateline>MONTERREY, Mexico 1997-02-14</dateline>
<text>
<p>Mexico's Hog Farmers Council scolded the Agriculture and Trade ministries Friday for not putting enough pressure on the U.S. government to allow hog exports from zones declared free of swine fever.</p>
<p>Council President Jose Cervera said the lack of the movement on the United States' part has hurt the industry and forced many farms to close down.</p>
<p>The United States agreed under the North American Free Agreement (NAFTA) it would recognise swine fever free zones in Mexico.</p>
<p>&quot;There has been a lack of firmness by our authorities. We have already asked a Binational Panel to discuss the U.S. commitment, from more than three years ago, which it has not done,&quot; Cervera told Reuters.</p>
<p>((--Jacqueline Lerma, Monterrey bureau, +528 3457677))</p>
